Hello, Serena Russo! 😊 The Picture-in-Picture feature in browsers like Firefox, Edge, and Opera can really interfere with the control you're trying to maintain over your SCORM packages. PiP allows the video to be played outside the main browser, which compromises the restrictions on fast-forwarding and rewinding, as you mentioned. But there are a few options to resolve this and ensure that users complete their lessons properly. Unfortunately, Moodle doesn't have a built-in setting to globally disable PiP across all browsers. However, we can force the video playback behavior by adding some custom code to the theme or directly within the SCORM files. One way to disable PiP is to block it via JavaScript . Add the following code to your Moodle theme (or, if possible, directly into the SCORM package within Storyline 360): 
```text
document.querySelectorAll('video').forEach(video => {
    video.disablePictureInPicture = true;
});
```
 This script prevents PiP from being activated on any video on the page and should be added directly as a JS script within the SCORM packages. 🎥 I hope these tips help you resolve the issue and ensure your course time reports are accurate! If you have any questions, we're here to help! 🌟 Eduardo Kraus Teacher and programmer

Based on the script from https://github.com/edwinp-catalyst-eu/scorm-migration-cli/blob/master/migrate_scorm.php as a basis, I wrote a PHP-based cronjob for our Moodle 3.8.3+ (build 20200527) install that scans the contents of <moodledata>/repository/content and automatically extracts the package via scorm_parse() if it was found to be updated. I modified the original script to include other calls used in the scorm_update_instance() function in mod/scorm/lib.php, which is called when a SCORM package is added via the UI. The relevant parts of the code are as follows: 
```text
scorm_parse($scorm, true);


scorm_update_grades($scorm);


scorm_update_calendar($scorm, $cmid);
```
 The problem I'm having is that on the initial run, scorm_update_grades() is performing a SQL UPDATE to mdl_grade_grades and it constantly hangs. Executing SHOW FULL PROCESSLIST on the server shows that the UPDATE statement is blocked waiting for a transaction ("waiting for handler commit") to complete, but darned if I can't figure out where the transaction is being initiated or how to force it to complete.  This causes my outer script to take a ridiculous amount of time per SCORM package: updating 700+ SCORM packages has taken over three days running non-stop.  The same hanging happens in the UI as well. Another strange thing is that if I run the script again without making a change to the packages, any that were imported via the script completes the grade updates in just a few seconds and do not exhibit the same behavior around transactions. Two questions: What on earth is going on with the grade updates & transactions? Since we don't actually use any of the grading or calendar functionality in our environment, would those be safe to ignore when updating the packages? (e.g. remove the scorm_update_grades() and scorm_update_calendar() function calls) Thank you!

